Command vs Entrypoint
Command: All image file will have command ie after creating image when you run what it has to perform that we will mention in cmd.
By default ubuntu will have bash on cmd  and as soon as we run ubuntu it end because it dose not run any process and eventhough we give bash in cmd it wont open terminal becuase docker dont open terminal by default. So it will exit asoon as it run. Below command we mention to run the ubuntu container for 10 sec, We can explicitly pass the command as well, so it run on bash. 
docker run ubuntu sleep 10
If we give like above and and inside our own image we wronte like this , what it will do is it will overwrite 5 sec of sleep to 10.
FROM ubuntu
CMD ["sleep", "5] - We need to mention in json format only. CMD ["command", "arg"]

There are some case i dont want mention everytime docker run ubuntu sleep 10 like this instead docker run ubuntu 10, what entrypoint will do is it will append with command ie sleep 10.
FROM ubuntu
ENTRYPOINT ["sleep"]

If there is case if i just missed to mention 10 then it will throw error in trhat case we can do like below, if we dont metion it will take cmd 5 and append to entrypoint and run. bestpractice always use both.
FROM ubuntu
ENTRYPOINT ["sleep"]
CMD ["5"]

If we want to give some different value to entrypoint can do like using --entrypoint, here sleep 2.0 will pass to sleep.
docker run --entrypoint sleep 2.0 ubuntu-sleeper 10

